 "notes": [
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>The salmon, shiso and sesame are folded through so the rice is pink and green all the way.</li><li>The ikura stays a pocket in the middle — one pop of red caviar, not beads mashed through every grain.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Salmon through the rice, ikura in the center"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Hot rice cooks the eggs soft and cloudy.</li><li>Cool the mixed rice until you can hold it, then spoon the cold ikura into the center.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Ikura cold, rice only warm"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Traditional salted salmon is cooked well done and dry, so it keeps in a lunch box.</li><li>For eating the same day, take it off as soon as it flakes — the flakes stay juicy in the rice.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Juicy salmon"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Salmon flakes from a Japanese grocery (shake flakes in a jar) work in place of grilling your own.</li><li>Ikura is sold in small trays, often frozen; thaw it in the fridge.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Store-bought shortcuts"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Shio kombu and the salt on your hands both salt the rice — taste a bit of the mixed rice before shaping and go lighter on your hands if it's already well seasoned.</li><li>Table salt: half the amount.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Salt"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>The mixed rice (salmon, shiso, myoga, shio kombu, sesame), its amounts and the shaping are Just One Cookbook's salmon and shio kombu onigiri.</li><li>The sake and salt on the fish and the fish grill are her shiozake, but shortened: she cures it 2 days and cooks it well done; here it's salted 20–30 minutes and taken off while still moist.</li><li>The <b>ikura in the center</b>, the <b>chopped crisp skin</b> and the <b>nori strip</b> are this site's additions.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Assumed: what's the source and what's mine"
  }
 ],
